window.PhoenixSiteSearchIndex={"generated_at_utc":"2026-10-28T01:07:33.524843Z","count":4,"pages":[{"title":"Why Cities Run Hot: The Urban Heat Island, Explained","url":"/why-cities-run-hot/","description":"On a summer afternoon, the middle of a large city is almost always warmer than the fields just beyond its edge.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Cities Run Hot: The Urban Heat Island, Explained Why Cities Run Hot: The Urban Heat Island, Explained Why Cities Run Hot: The Urban Heat Island, Explained Why Cities Run Hot: The Urban Heat Island, Explained Why Cities Run Hot: The Urban Heat Island, Explained On a summer afternoon, the middle of a large city is almost always warmer than the fields just beyond its edge. On a summer afternoon, the middle of a large city is almost always warmer than the fields just beyond its edge. This is not an illusion of traffic and noise: instruments measure it reliably, satellites photograph it from orbit, and electricity grids pay for it every hot afternoon. Why Cities Run Hot why cities run hot Updated 2026 10 28 Introduction In the United States, the daytime air in cities runs roughly 1–7°F (about 0.5–4°C) warmer than in surrounding areas, and at night the gap narrows but persists at about 2–5°F.[1] Those averages hide the real story. Between one city block and the next, afternoon temperatures can differ by several degrees, and the hottest neighbourhoods are systematically the ones with the least vegetation and the most pavement. Heat is not distributed evenly by accident, and it is not experienced evenly either: around 489,000 heat related deaths are recorded worldwide each year, and the toll climbs during the heatwaves that climate change is making longer and hotter.[2] This article explains what a heat island is, why it forms, how strong it is in practice, and why it matters for health, energy and fairness. It is the first in a series: the articles that follow examine the two main families of remedies — reflective surfaces and urban greenery — and the maps and institutions cities use to manage heat deliberately rather than accidentally. Where to next Cool Roofs and Reflective Surfaces: Turning Down the Urban Thermostat Mapping the Heat: How Cities See, Predict and Manage Dangerous Temperatures Trees, Canopy and Green Space: A City's Natural Air Conditioning Illustration 1 Urban Heat Island Magnitude Two kinds of heat island Researchers distinguish two related measurements that are often confused.[1] Surface heat islands describe the temperature of the ground and building materials themselves — asphalt, roofs, car parks — as satellites and infrared cameras see them. Surface differences are the largest by day: dark, unshaded pavement can exceed the air temperature above it by tens of degrees. Conventional roofing on a warm day can run as much as 66°F (about 37°C) hotter than the surrounding air.[1] Atmospheric heat islands describe the air temperature people actually feel, measured at standard height. The air signal is smaller than the surface signal but steadier: it builds through the day, peaks a few hours after sunset, and persists into the night because buildings and streets release stored heat slowly.[1] The distinction matters for policy. A city can have dramatic satellite images of hot pavement while its worst public health problem is the night time air temperature in dense districts where people cannot cool their bedrooms. The two problems share causes but not always the same fixes. YouTube How urban heat islands make the impacts of excessive heat worse Open on YouTube How urban heat islands make the impacts of excessive heat worse Channel: PBS NewsHour Open on YouTube Why cities run hot: the energy balance The physical explanation, worked out in foundational work by T. R. Oke and refined since, is that cities alter the surface energy balance in several ways at once.[3] First, cities replace vegetation with sealed, dark surfaces. A forest or a wet meadow spends a large share of the sun's energy on evaporation — turning liquid water into vapour, which cools the surface. Asphalt and membrane roofing have no water to evaporate, so nearly all the absorbed sunlight becomes sensible heat: warmth you can feel. Second, urban materials differ in their radiative properties. Dark surfaces absorb more solar radiation, and deep, massive materials store that energy effectively during the day and release it after sunset. Third, urban geometry traps both sun and heat. Street canyons — corridors of tall buildings — reflect radiation back and forth between walls, block the cool evening sky from view, and shelter the air from wind. In calm, clear summer weather, when heatwaves are at their worst, this trapping is strongest.[1][3] Fourth, cities generate their own heat. Vehicles, air conditioning condensers, industry and lighting all dump waste heat directly into the urban air — and the hotter the city gets, the harder the air conditioners work, a feedback loop measured on power grids as a 1–9% rise in electricity demand for every 2°F of warming.[1] Archive map Global Urban Heat Island (UHI) Data Set, 2013 Summer Nighttime Urban Rural Temperature Difference (30563444636) SEDACMaps · Global Urban Heat Island (UHI) Data Set, 2013: Summer Nighttime Urban Rural Temperature Difference · 2016 10 27 10:24 · 85086842 Selected from approved repository metadata by deterministic subject overlap. CC BY 2.0 · View original record How strong is the effect in real cities? The heat island is not an exotic laboratory effect; it is one of the most consistently observed features of urban climate. A synthesis of one hundred large Asian and Australian cities found that nearly all exhibit clear heat islands, with intensities that vary with city size, building density and background climate, and that tend to peak in the evening.[4] Reviews of European cities tell the same story. Within a single city, block scale differences are striking. A bicycle mounted measurement campaign that sampled air temperature every five metres across a mid sized American city found that daytime temperature variability within the urban landscape averaged 3.5°C (range 1.1–5.7°C) on summer days — a range comparable to the difference between a mild and a scorching afternoon forecast for the whole region.[5] In other words, two residents of the same city, on the same day, can live in materially different climates depending on the design of their street. The effect is strongest in larger, denser cities and in humid regions; wind and clouds suppress it.[1] Paradoxically, the heat island is most dangerous precisely on the calm, clear days of a heatwave, when regional temperatures are already extreme and nothing disperses the city's extra heat. Illustration 2 Redlining Legacy vs. Modern Heat Heat, health and the night problem Extreme heat is among the deadliest of weather hazards, and heat islands amplify it. Between 2000 and 2019, about 489,000 people died each year from heat related causes, with 45% of those deaths in Asia and 36% in Europe.[2] Europe's 2003 summer heatwave killed an estimated 70,000 people; the Russian heatwave of 2010 an estimated 56,000 in forty four days; and the summer of 2022 was linked to more than 61,000 excess deaths across Europe despite half a century of warnings and modern air conditioning.[2] Heat mortality in adults over 65 rose roughly 85% between 2000–2004 and 2017–2021 as populations aged and heatwaves intensified.[2] The people most at risk are well described: older adults living alone, people with cardiovascular, respiratory, kidney and mental health conditions, outdoor and manual workers, infants, and urban poor whose housing offers no relief.[2] Within cities, informal settlements and neighbourhoods without shade or cooling are systematically hotter — cities, as the World Health Organization notes, \"are not being designed to minimise the accumulation and generation of urban heat\", and their building choices amplify exposure.[2] Night time heat is the particular killer. Human bodies need cooler nights to recover from hot days; consecutive hot nights raise mortality sharply. Because urban materials release stored heat after sunset, the atmospheric heat island is typically strongest at night, landing exactly where it does the most physiological harm.[1][2][3] Beyond death, heat degrades city life in quieter ways: it raises ground level ozone, which forms faster in warm sunlight and aggravates asthma; it pushes electricity grids toward brownouts on hot afternoons; and it heats urban streams — stormwater running off hot pavement has been measured as much as 18°F warmer than runoff from forested land, stressing aquatic ecosystems.[1] YouTube How America's hottest city is trying to cool down Open on YouTube How America's hottest city is trying to cool down Channel: Vox Open on YouTube The heat is unequally distributed Heat islands are not socially neutral. A national analysis of the 175 largest US cities found that people of colour, on average, live with higher urban heat island intensity than white residents — a pattern that holds in the overwhelming majority of cities studied and is compounded by income.[6] The roots are often historical. A study of 108 US urban areas showed that neighbourhoods graded \"hazardous\" in the 1930s federal redlining maps — typically"},{"title":"Cool Roofs and Reflective Surfaces: Turning Down the...","url":"/cool-roofs-and-reflective-surfaces/","description":"A roof is a decision about sunlight. Every square metre of conventional dark roofing absorbs most of the solar energy that strikes it and converts that...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Cool Roofs and Reflective Surfaces: Turning Down the... Cool Roofs and Reflective Surfaces: Turning Down the Urban Thermostat Cool Roofs and Reflective Surfaces: Turning Down the... Cool Roofs and Reflective Surfaces: Turning Down the... Cool Roofs and Reflective Surfaces: Turning Down the Urban Thermostat A roof is a decision about sunlight. Every square metre of conventional dark roofing absorbs most of the solar energy that strikes it and converts that... A roof is a decision about sunlight. Every square metre of conventional dark roofing absorbs most of the solar energy that strikes it and converts that energy into heat — heat that flows into the rooms below, into the air above, and into the electricity bill of the building's owner. Cool Roofs cool roofs Updated 2026 10 28 Introduction A cool roof makes the opposite decision. By reflecting more sunlight and shedding the heat it does absorb, it stays dramatically cooler, lowers indoor temperatures in buildings without air conditioning, cuts peak cooling demand where air conditioning exists, and — deployed across thousands of buildings — measurably cools the city around it. Cool roofing is one of the few urban climate strategies that is simultaneously a retrofit (a bucket of coating), a building code, and a global climate lever.[1][2] This article, the second in the Cool Cities series on urban heat, explains how reflective surfaces work, what they achieve at building and city scale, where their limits are, and why codes and rebate programmes now treat roof colour as public infrastructure. Where to next Mapping the Heat: How Cities See, Predict and Manage Dangerous Temperatures Trees, Canopy and Green Space: A City's Natural Air Conditioning Why Cities Run Hot: The Urban Heat Island, Explained Illustration 1 Cool Roof Physics & Impact The physics: reflect it, or re emit it Two properties determine how hot a roof runs in the sun.[1] Solar reflectance — albedo — is the fraction of incoming sunlight a surface bounces away. A fresh dark membrane reflects very little; it absorbs most sunlight and becomes a radiator. A bright white coating can reflect the large majority of solar energy, which never becomes heat at all. Reflectance is the dominant factor in how hot the surface gets. Thermal emittance is how efficiently a surface re radiates the heat it does absorb, mainly as infrared energy. A high emittance surface dumps stored heat back toward the sky quickly, which matters most in warm, sunny climates and especially on calm clear nights. The two together explain surface temperatures: a material that neither reflects nor emits well becomes the hottest object in the landscape. This is why dark, unshaded asphalt and bituminous roofing dominate thermal images of cities.[1][3] YouTube Cooling the Earth, One Roof at a Time Open on YouTube Cooling the Earth, One Roof at a Time Channel: Associated Press Open on YouTube What a cool roof is, in practice Cool roofing is a product category, not a single material, and it exists for both flat commercial roofs and pitched residential ones.[1] On low slope roofs (flatter than about 2:12), the standard options include liquid applied reflective coatings; single ply membranes made with cool coloured or white sheeting; light coloured gravel aggregate ballast; and reflective granule surfaced asphaltic membranes. On steep slope residential roofs, manufacturers now offer asphalt shingles made with infrared reflective granules that look like ordinary colours but reflect the near infrared portion of sunlight — the majority of solar energy — that conventional pigments absorb. Terra cotta tiles are naturally cool; glazed and polymer coated tiles, reflective metal shingles, and composite shingles with cool pigments extend the palette. So called \"directionally reflective\" products even change apparent colour with viewing angle to balance reflectance against appearance. Maintenance matters: low slope roofs need periodic cleaning because soiling erodes reflectance over time, and products with special reflective pigments carry a price premium.[1] Place NASA SLC Urban Heat Island Effect Roof NASA · Taken from Cool Roofing: A Solution to National Energy and Environmental Challenges. A Self guided presentation for AIA HSW and Sustainable Design Credit. · 16:16, 27 August 2010 (UTC) · 146457500 Selected from approved repository metadata by deterministic subject overlap. Public domain · View original record What the measurements show, building by building The US Environmental Protection Agency's synthesis of field studies quantifies the indoor effects.[1] In residential buildings without air conditioning, a cool roof lowers maximum indoor temperatures by 1.2–3.3°C (2.2–5.9°F) — often the difference between a bedroom that recovers at night and one that does not. In air conditioned residential buildings, the reflectance gain reduces peak cooling demand by 11–27%. Because utility grids strain at the peak — and because electricity for cooling is itself a source of urban waste heat — this demand reduction compounds: every cooled building dumps less heat into the street. These numbers echo the longer research literature. Since foundational work in the late 1990s and early 2000s established that cool surfaces and shade trees measurably cut building cooling loads and urban air temperatures, field and modelling studies have repeatedly confirmed the pattern across climates.[3] Illustration 2 Roof Material Options & Limits From one roof to a whole city The more consequential effects appear when reflective roofing is combined with lighter pavement across a district or city. This is where individual retrofits become climate policy.[2][4] A widely cited global analysis by Hashem Akbari, Surabi Menon and Arthur Rosenfeld calculated that increasing the albedo of urban roofs and pavements in tropical and temperate cities worldwide — a realistic retrofit of the existing stock — would produce a one time offset of about 44 billion tonnes of CO2, because reflecting solar energy back to space counteracts a portion of greenhouse forcing. Follow up modelling of the radiative forcing from urban albedo changes confirmed a small but genuinely global cooling effect, alongside the large local ones.[2][4] The health case is at least as strong as the climate case. Modelling for the West Midlands of England found that city wide cool roofs could offset roughly 18% of the heat related mortality attributable to the urban heat island during heatwaves — a figure the EPA highlights because it converts roof colour directly into saved lives.[1][5] And because ground level ozone forms faster in hot air, cooler cities also tend to have cleaner air on the days that matter most.[1] YouTube Energy 101: Cool Roofs Open on YouTube Energy 101: Cool Roofs Channel: U.S. Department of Energy Open on YouTube The honest limits Cool surfaces are not free lunch, and a rigorous treatment acknowledges the costs. The winter penalty is real but usually small: a reflective roof gives up some winter solar gain that would have offset heating. In most climates studied, the penalty is outweighed by summer savings because winter sun is weaker, days are shorter, and snow already whitens many cold city roofs.[1] Follow up UK modelling that compared cool roofs' summer and winter mortality effects across a full year still found substantial net benefit in heat exposed districts.[6] Soiling and ageing erode reflectance — a white roof is not white forever without cleaning or recoating. Some reflective coatings carry upfront costs above conventional products, though utility rebate programmes in eleven US states now offset much of that difference.[1] Glare is the most cited neighbourhood objection: a low slope white roof visible from upper floors can disturb neighbours, which is one reason cool coloured (rather than bright white) products matter for acceptance. And cool roofs do nothing for stormwater, biodiversity, air quality or amenity — benefits that vegetation provides and pavement colour changes cannot, a comparison developed in the third article of this series. Illustration 3 Policy & Incentive Landscape Codes, rebates and the policy machine Because a roof is replaced only every few decades, policy concentrates on the replacement moment. As of the EPA's most recent count, cool roof requirements are mandatory in at least 13 US cities and counties and seven states plus the District of Columbia, largely for new and re roofed low slope buildings. Thirty five utility and municipal rebate programmes across eleven states subsidise reflective roofing, making incentives the most common instrument. Eight voluntary programmes, including LEED, recognise cool roofs as well.[1] The strategy has spread well beyond the United States. Coating programmes in Indian cities, requirements in European building codes, and the iconic whitewashed roofs of Greek islands — vernacular architecture that anticipated the physics by centuries — all express the same"},{"title":"Trees, Canopy and Green Space: A City's Natural Air...","url":"/trees-and-canopy/","description":"Vegetation cools cities the way it cooled the land before cities existed: by turning sunlight into water vapour instead of heat, and by casting shade that...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Trees, Canopy and Green Space: A City's Natural Air... Trees, Canopy and Green Space: A City's Natural Air Conditioning Trees, Canopy and Green Space: A City's Natural Air... Trees, Canopy and Green Space: A City's Natural Air... Trees, Canopy and Green Space: A City's Natural Air Conditioning Vegetation cools cities the way it cooled the land before cities existed: by turning sunlight into water vapour instead of heat, and by casting shade that... Vegetation cools cities the way it cooled the land before cities existed: by turning sunlight into water vapour instead of heat, and by casting shade that interrupts the sun before pavement can absorb it. Trees and Canopy trees and canopy Updated 2026 10 28 Introduction The evidence for greenery's cooling effect is unusually direct. It can be measured with a thermometer on a bicycle. It appears in satellite records of every leafy city. It is visible in the mortality data of heatwaves, when neighbourhoods with canopy fare better than neighbourhoods without. And unlike most urban infrastructure, a tree's cooling capacity grows every year it lives.[2][4] Where to next Cool Roofs and Reflective Surfaces: Turning Down the... Mapping the Heat: How Cities See, Predict and Manage Dangerous Temperatures Why Cities Run Hot: The Urban Heat Island, Explained This article, the third in the Cool Cities series, examines what trees, canopy and vegetated roofs actually do to city temperatures, at what scale, for whom — and what it takes to keep the benefits alive through drought, budget cycles and decades of growth. Illustration 1 Canopy Cooling Benchmarks Two mechanisms: shade and evapotranspiration A tree cools its surroundings in two ways at once.[3] Shade is the simpler half. Leaves intercept sunlight before it strikes asphalt, roofs or a person's skin. Shaded surfaces stay dramatically cooler than unshaded ones because the energy never becomes heat in the material in the first place; this is also why a tree over a street cools the street more than a tree in an open lawn. For a pedestrian, shade can be the difference between walkable and dangerous on a hot afternoon. Evapotranspiration is the subtler half. A tree is a water pump: roots draw soil moisture and leaves release it as vapour through microscopic stomata. Converting liquid water to vapour consumes energy — the latent heat of vaporisation — so every litre transpired is energy that does not become air temperature. A single mature tree can transpire hundreds of litres on a hot day, functioning as a continuously running, solar powered air conditioner with no peak load surcharge. The two mechanisms interact with urban form. In street canyons, canopy shades both roadway and walls while transpiring above; the cooling reaches pedestrians directly. In parks, the combination cools a volume of air that then drifts into surrounding blocks. YouTube The cooling effects of urban tree canopies Open on YouTube The cooling effects of urban tree canopies Channel: CBC British Columbia Open on YouTube What the measurements show The clearest modern evidence comes from a study that sampled air temperature every five metres along repeated bicycle transects across a mid sized American city in summer. Its findings set the quantitative agenda for urban forestry.[2] First, within city daytime temperature variability averaged 3.5°C (range 1.1–5.7°C) — block to block differences as large as regional climate differences. Second, the relationship between canopy and temperature is non linear: cooling accelerates once tree canopy cover exceeds roughly 40%, meaning scattered individual trees deliver less cooling per unit of canopy than continuous, connected canopy. Third, the cooling effect is strongest at the scale of a city block (60–90 m), which is exactly the scale of a street, a schoolyard or a housing estate. And fourth, impervious cover warmed air linearly — but the warming from pavement was weaker than the cooling from canopy. Trees are not a garnish; at sufficient cover they are the dominant surface control on daytime air temperature.[2] At the larger scale of parks, a systematic review of the empirical evidence found that urban parks are, on average, about 1°C cooler than their built up surroundings by day, with the strongest park cool islands in larger, greener parks, and effects that extend into the neighbouring streets for some distance.[5] Night time park effects can be larger still, as parks shed the day's heat faster than adjacent buildings. Place 20080708 Chicago City Hall Green Roof TonyTheTiger · I created this work entirely by myself. TonyTheTiger · 2008 07 08 · 5236408 Selected from approved repository metadata by deterministic subject overlap. CC BY SA 3.0 · View original record Not all green is equal: canopy versus turf An important subtlety for planners: the bicycle measurement study and others consistently find that it is tree canopy, not general \"greenness\", that drives daytime cooling. A mown lawn of turfgrass transpires little and shades nothing; a closed canopy does both. Cities that count any vegetated cover toward greening targets can hit their numbers while leaving afternoon temperatures untouched.[2] Species and placement matter as well. Dense, broad crowns over streets and west facing walls deliver the most useful shade; trees over permeable, irrigated soil transpire most reliably. In dry climates, the water cost of that transpiration must be counted honestly — unirrigated trees under heat and drought stress close their stomata and stop cooling precisely on the hottest days, which is why drought tolerant species, soil volume and watering budgets are part of cooling policy, not gardening trivia. Green roofs and walls: vegetation as building skin Where ground space is exhausted, vegetation can climb the building itself. Green roofs — extensive sedum mats or deeper intensive plantings — insulate, shade, and transpire on what would otherwise be a city's hottest surface. According to the EPA's synthesis, green roof surfaces can be up to 56°F cooler than conventional roofs, green roofs can reduce nearby air temperatures by up to 20°F, and they can cut a building's cooling load dramatically while lowering indoor air temperatures and slowing stormwater runoff by 60% or more in smaller storms.[6] Green (vegetated) facades and trellised walls apply the same principle vertically, shading walls that absorb strongly in the afternoon sun. The stormwater and biodiversity co benefits distinguish vegetated surfaces from reflective ones: a cool roof lowers temperature and nothing else, while a green roof lowers temperature, retains rain, hosts insects and birds, and extends roof membrane life by shielding it from ultraviolet degradation.[6] Illustration 2 Shade vs Evapotranspiration Cooling that protects health — when it reaches people Canopy's public health value concentrates in three mechanisms: it lowers the air temperature people breathe, it lowers the radiant temperature people feel (shade), and it makes streets walkable during heatwaves, which keeps cooling centres, clinics and water reachable on foot. But the distribution of canopy is deeply unequal, and it tracks the same historical lines as heat itself. The national analysis of 108 US urban areas that linked 1930s redlining maps to present day land surface temperature found formerly redlined neighbourhoods hotter in the great majority of cases — and those same neighbourhoods, shaped by decades of disinvestment, are typically the ones with the least mature tree canopy.[4] A companion analysis across the largest US cities found people of colour live with higher heat island intensity on average than white residents of the same cities.[7] The policy consequence is that tree planting has become equity infrastructure. Planting programmes from New York to Barcelona now prioritise by heat and canopy gap rather than by volunteer convenience. Because a sapling takes ten to twenty years to deliver meaningful shade, the canopy a city plants — or fails to plant — this decade defines the heat exposure of the next. YouTube How Green Roofs Can Help Cities NPR Open on YouTube How Green Roofs Can Help Cities NPR Channel: NPR Open on YouTube Keeping the forest alive: maintenance as policy Urban trees die young when treated as decoration. Compacted soil, road salt, drought, root space starvation and budget cuts each kill trees that codes and campaigns plant. The cooling literature is blunt that sustained canopy requires sustained care: irrigation in the establishment years, species diversity so a single pest cannot erase a generation of planting (the fate of elm dominated cities under Dutch elm disease), soil volume compatible with maturity, and pruning that preserves crowns rather than mutilating them for utility clearance.[3] The arithmetic of maintenance is unforgiving in the other direction too: mature canopy is disproportionately valuable. Removing a 70 year old street tree eliminates decades of accumulated"},{"title":"Mapping the Heat: How Cities See, Predict and Manage...","url":"/mapping-the-heat/","description":"You cannot cool what you cannot see. For most of the twentieth century a city knew its temperature from a single airport sensor — an average over an airfield...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Mapping the Heat: How Cities See, Predict and Manage... Mapping the Heat: How Cities See, Predict and Manage Dangerous Temperatures Mapping the Heat: How Cities See, Predict and Manage... Mapping the Heat: How Cities See, Predict and Manage... Mapping the Heat: How Cities See, Predict and Manage Dangerous Temperatures You cannot cool what you cannot see. For most of the twentieth century a city knew its temperature from a single airport sensor — an average over an airfield... You cannot cool what you cannot see. For most of the twentieth century a city knew its temperature from a single airport sensor — an average over an airfield that resembled nothing in the city it nominally described. Mapping the Heat mapping the heat Updated 2026 10 28 Introduction The answers reorganised heat policy. Temperature in a city varies block by block by several degrees — the same magnitude as decades of regional warming — and the hottest blocks map onto the least powerful residents with uncomfortable precision.[3][4][5] That discovery converted heat from a weather problem, which a city can only endure, into a design and governance problem, which a city can act on. Where to next Cool Roofs and Reflective Surfaces: Turning Down the... Trees, Canopy and Green Space: A City's Natural Air... Why Cities Run Hot: The Urban Heat Island, Explained This article, the fourth in the Cool Cities series, follows the machinery of that shift: the satellites that photograph heat from orbit, the volunteers who drive sensors through their own neighbourhoods, the indices that combine temperature with vulnerability, and the institutions — heat officers, heat action plans, early warning systems — that turn maps into intervention. Illustration 1 From Orbit to Street Level Seeing heat from orbit Thermal satellites transformed the study of urban heat by making surface temperature visible everywhere, repeatedly, for free. Instruments such as Landsat's thermal band map land surface temperature at a resolution of tens of metres, which is fine enough to distinguish a motorway from the park beside it. NASA's ECOSTRESS instrument, mounted on the International Space Station, images cities at roughly 70 metre resolution at different times of day, capturing both the afternoon build up of surface heat and — more valuable for health — how slowly different districts cool after dark.[6] Two disciplines keep satellite heat maps honest. Surface temperature is not air temperature: satellites see roofs and roads, not the air people breathe, and the relationship between the two varies with materials and time of day. And a single image is a moment: comparisons between cities, or years, require consistent processing of what the research literature calls surface urban heat islands.[1][6] What the images show, consistently, is structure. Thermal images of virtually any large city reveal a rings and corriders pattern — hot industrial and parking districts, cool parks and waterways, warm dense cores, cooler leafy suburbs — that matches the material and canopy analysis of the earlier articles in this series.[1][3] YouTube 60 volunteers to participate in citywide heat mapping study Open on YouTube 60 volunteers to participate in citywide heat mapping study Channel: News4JAX The Local Station Open on YouTube Ground truth: sensors on bicycles and car windows Satellites see surfaces; people experience air. The most influential recent air temperature datasets come from simple instruments moved through the city. In Portland, Oregon, researchers laid the methodological groundwork by combining dense mobile measurements with distributed sensor networks to document systematic intra urban variation and to ground truth the models used to predict it.[7] The method has since been scaled into civic campaigns. In community heat mapping programmes, volunteers mount sensors on their cars and bicycles and drive set routes in the cool of the morning, the heat of the afternoon and the evening, transecting their own city in a single coordinated day. The result is a three dimensional picture of heat — which districts heat first, hottest and longest — at street level, produced by the residents who live with the result. Local news coverage of these campaigns, from Salt Lake City to Montgomery County, shows the consistent findings: simultaneous differences of roughly 5–15°F between the coolest and hottest parts of the same city, on the same afternoon, in the same weather.[8][9] The measurement campaigns settle an argument that averages obscure: the intra city spread rivals the projected regional warming of the coming decades. A city can, in effect, buy back climate decades for its worst blocks with shade and reflectance. Archive evidence NASA's ECOSTRESS Detects ‘Heat Islands' in Extreme Indian Heat Wave NASA/JPL Caltech · Wikimedia Commons · 2022 05 05 · 117958973 Selected from approved repository metadata by deterministic subject overlap. Public domain · View original record From temperature to vulnerability: the heat equity map A heat map becomes policy when temperature is combined with the social data that determines who heat kills. Heat vulnerability indices layer measured or modelled temperature onto the demographics of risk: age, chronic illness, social isolation, air conditioning access, housing quality, occupation.[2][4][5] The findings of that literature are among the most robust in urban climate. Analysis across the 175 largest US cities found people of colour live with higher heat island intensity than white residents in the overwhelming majority of cities, a pattern that persists within income groups.[5] The historical study of 108 urban areas showed 1930s redlining grades still predict present day surface temperature: formerly redlined neighbourhoods are hotter in the large majority of cases, by up to about 7°C in the most extreme cities, reflecting decades of pavement heavy, canopy poor investment.[4] Vulnerability mapping does more than document injustice; it directs resources. Tree planting, cool roof rebates, cooling centres and outreach all cost money, and indices decide whether they flow to the blocks that need them or to the blocks that ask loudest. Illustration 2 Heat Equity and History Institutions: heat officers and heat action plans Maps need someone to answer them. The institutional response has developed along three lines. Early warning systems and heat action plans are the established core. A heat action plan defines the triggers (temperature forecasts, humidity, duration), the alerts, and the response ladder: opening cooling centres, extending pool and park hours, checking on isolated residents, protecting outdoor workers, and shifting clinic and utility practice. The World Health Organization treats heat–health action plans and early warning systems as core adaptation infrastructure, alongside guidance for health institutions.[2] The world's most cited early plan is Ahmedabad, in India, adopted after a devastating 2010 heatwave; evaluations credit it with measurable reductions in heat mortality, while subsequent analysis emphasises that a plan must keep renewing itself — thresholds age as the climate warms, and initial funding attention fades.[10] Dedicated heat governance is the newer layer. Beginning in 2021 with the appointment of the world's first chief heat officers in Miami Dade County and Athens, cities have begun assigning executive responsibility for heat — someone whose job is to notice that June broke records and to make the bureaucracy respond. Athens' first chief heat officer, Eleni Myrivili, has described the three part logic of modern heat governance: name and rank heatwaves so the public treats them as emergencies, cool the city with shade and water and materials, and protect the most exposed residents with targeted outreach.[11] The model has since spread across Europe, North America and Africa. Neighbourhood scale intervention is the physical layer where mapping, equity and engineering converge: shade structures over waiting areas and schoolyards, cool roofs and cool corridors in the hottest census tracts, misting and drinking fountains, and rescue programmes for the outdoor workers whose exposure is occupational rather than incidental. YouTube Exploring Your City from Space: Part 4 Urban Heat Islands, Flooding & Canopy Open on YouTube Exploring Your City from Space: Part 4 Urban Heat Islands, Flooding & Canopy Channel: NASA Video Open on YouTube The discipline of follow through The hardest part of heat governance is not the map or the plan; it is the follow through. Evaluations of heat action plans repeatedly find the same failure modes: warning systems that reach smartphones but not the isolated elderly; cooling centres that open too late, close too early, or sit beyond a heat exhausted walk; plans written for a past climate whose trigger thresholds no longer distinguish dangerous from ordinary summers; and programmes funded by one grant cycle, staffed by none.[2][10] The"}]};
